course: everyday
lesson: 122
title: Greeting friends and relatives
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: When you see a friend, you say hi, use their name and smile; people feel happy when they are noticed and someone is glad to see them.
explain: Even in a bad mood you still say hello back, because ignoring a friend can hurt them; you can tell them later what is wrong.
explain: A small hello to a classmate you do not usually play with can make their day and start a new friendship.
explain: Relatives came to see you, so you stop what you are doing and greet them warmly; with someone who hears badly you face them and speak clearly, a little louder, but you do not shout.
explain: A girl in Sydney sees her cousin for the first time in two years and feels shy; she says hello and asks "What have you been doing?", and soon they are chatting as if they had never been apart.
fact: Saying hi, using a friend's name and smiling shows you are glad to see them.
fact: Ignoring a friend's hello can hurt them, even if you are upset about something else.
fact: A special handshake is fun with a close friend, but other people are greeted in an ordinary way.
fact: A kind hello to someone new can help you make new friends.
fact: In a busy corridor you walk closer or wave instead of shouting.
fact: When grandparents arrive, you stop what you are doing and go to greet them.
fact: Shyness passes quickly once you start talking, and a simple question helps.
fact: When a relative says "Look how much you have grown!", a smile and "Thank you" or "I know!" is a good answer.
fact: To someone who hears badly you face them and speak clearly and a bit louder, without shouting.
fact: Facing a person helps them read your lips.
fact: When a relative phones on your birthday, you thank them for remembering and tell them about your day.
exercise: You see your friend at the school gate. What is a good greeting? | Say hi, use their name and smile | Walk past and wait for them to speak first | Wave at someone else standing behind them | Pretend to be very busy with your bag
exercise: Your friend says hello, but you are in a bad mood. What should you do? | Still say hello back, and tell them later if something is wrong | Ignore them until your mood gets better | Tell them to go away and leave you alone | Walk off without a word so they understand how bad your day has been
exercise: You and your best friend have a secret handshake. How do you greet other people? | In an ordinary way, so nobody feels left out | With the secret handshake too, just to show them | Not at all, because the handshake is only for one friend | By teaching everyone the secret handshake at once
exercise: A classmate you do not usually play with says hello. What do you do? | Say hello back kindly | Look away, because you are not friends | Ask why they are talking to you | Say hello only if your friends are not watching
exercise: You want to greet a friend at the far end of a busy corridor. What is best? | Wave or walk closer instead of shouting | Shout their name as loudly as you possibly can | Whistle loudly until they finally turn around | Throw a paper ball to get their attention
exercise: Your grandparents arrive to visit. What is polite? | Stop what you are doing and go to greet them warmly | Finish your game first and then greet them later at dinner time | Shout hello from your room | Wait for them to come and find you
exercise: You meet a cousin you have not seen for a long time and feel shy. What helps? | Say hello and ask something simple, like what they have been doing | Stay silent until the shyness goes away by itself | Hide in another room until they leave | Let your parents do all of the talking for you until the shyness is gone
exercise: Your aunt says, "Look how much you have grown!" What is a polite reply? | Smile and say "Thank you" or "I know!" | Roll your eyes at her and say nothing at all | Sigh and say "Everyone always says that" | Tell her that she looks a lot older now too
exercise: Your great-grandmother hears badly. How do you talk to her? | Face her and speak clearly and a bit louder, but do not shout | Shout as loudly as you can right into her ear so she hears every word | Talk quickly so she hears more words | Whisper and hope she understands
exercise: Why does facing someone who hears badly help? | They can read your lips and catch your words better | It makes your voice travel twice as far across the room | It stops other noises in the room | It makes their hearing aid work faster
exercise: Your uncle phones on your birthday. What do you do? | Thank him for remembering and tell him about your day | Say "Thanks" and hang up quickly | Ask him what present he is sending and when it will arrive | Tell your mum to say you are busy
exercise: Why do we answer a friend's hello even when we are upset? | Ignoring a friend can hurt them, even if they did nothing wrong | Because otherwise the teacher will give a detention | Because a hello always fixes a bad mood | Because friends never notice when you ignore them
test: A boy in Toronto is cross because he lost a game, and his friend Ella says "Hi!" What is best? | Say "Hi, Ella" and explain later that he is cross about the game | Turn away without a word so that Ella can see how cross he really is | Tell Ella it is her fault | Say nothing until tomorrow
test: Why is a hello to a lonely new classmate worth it? | A small greeting can make someone's day and start a friendship | Because the teacher secretly gives points for every hello to new pupils | Because new pupils always become best friends | Because it makes you more popular than them
test: Which is the best way to talk to a grandparent who wears a hearing aid? | Face them and speak clearly, a little louder | Speak from the next room so they practise | Shout every word as loudly as possible | Mumble quickly to finish sooner
test: Your granddad rings the doorbell while you are playing a video game. What do you do? | Pause the game and go to the door to greet him | Shout "Come in!" and keep playing | Finish the level and then say hello | Ask your sister to go down and greet him for you while you play
test: A girl in Leeds and her best friend have a special greeting dance. A new girl is standing with them. What is kind? | Greet the new girl in an ordinary friendly way too | Do the dance and ignore the new girl | Tell the new girl the dance is secret and only for best friends | Wait until the new girl leaves to greet anyone
test: Your aunt in Ireland calls to say happy birthday. What will make her happiest? | Thanking her and telling her something about your day | Answering every question with "Fine" | Asking how much money she put in the birthday card this year | Passing the phone on straight away
test: Your cousin, whom you have not seen for years, looks shy too. What is a good first step? | Say hello and ask what they have been doing | Wait for the cousin to speak first | Play alone so nobody feels awkward | Tell your parents you are too shy to talk
test: Why should you not shout to a friend down the school corridor? | It is loud for everyone else and can disturb classes | Because shouting makes your voice break and you lose it for days | Because friends cannot hear shouting | Because it is the only rule that matters at school
test: nana's here! but im mid game | Pause it and go greet her | Wave from the sofa and keep playing | Finish the level first | Shout hi from your room
test: grandad cant hear me well, should i shout | Face him and speak clearly and a bit louder | Yes, shout as loud as you can, that is the only way he will hear | Whisper close to his ear | Speak fast so it's quick
test: auntie says look how much uve grown!! what do i even say | Smile and say thank you or I know | Say nothing and walk off | Say she has grown too | Tell her it's rude
test: new kid sat alone at lunch, say hi? | Yes, a kind hello can start a friendship | No, wait until they talk first | No, new kids always want to be left alone on their first day | Only if a teacher tells you
explain: A boy in Leeds hugged his cousin hard every time even though she said she did not like it; she started avoiding family visits until a grown-up explained he must ask first.
explain: A girl in Texas ignored her friends when they said hi because she was in a bad mood; by the end of the week they had stopped asking her to play.
fact: People have a right to say no to hugs, kisses or tickling, even from family, and others should respect it.
fact: Ignoring friends' greetings often makes them feel hurt and less likely to include you.
fact: Grown-ups' relationships, like a new girlfriend or a second marriage, are their private business, so you greet everyone politely and do not gossip.
test: auntie wants a kiss, i don't want to | It's ok to offer a wave or high five instead | You must always kiss family | Hide in the car until she leaves so she cannot ask again | Shout no and run off
test: in a mood, not saying hi to my mates today | Say a quick hi, ignoring them hurts their feelings | Ignore them all today, they'll get that you're in a mood | Glare at them instead | Tell them you hate them
test: my cousin always tickles me even when i say stop | Say stop clearly and tell an adult, your no counts | Keep laughing so he stops | Tickle him harder | Never visit again
test: did u hear Leo hugs his cousin every time even tho she says no, n now "she's never coming to xmas again". real lesson? | Ask first, people can say no to hugs even in family | Family hugs are always fine without asking | She should just get used to the hugs | Only kisses need asking, not hugs
test: apparently Grace ignored everyone for a week n "now she's got zero friends forever". what's probs exaggerated | Zero friends forever; but ignoring friends does hurt them | That ignoring friends' hellos for a week makes them feel hurt | That friends may stop asking her to play | That she can say sorry and say hi again
test: kids are saying Tom's nan is "deaf as a post n shouts all the time" lol. pass it on? | No, it is unkind to mock someone who hears badly | Yes, it is just a joke about old people | Yes, but only when Tom is not around | Yes, because she really cannot hear it
exam: Identify the principal manner of speaking appropriate to a relative who hears with difficulty. | Face them and speak clearly, a bit louder | Shout loudly from the next room over | Whisper close behind their back | Speak quickly so it takes less time
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning the arrival of grandparents at the family home. | One stops what one is doing to greet them | One carries on playing until they come over | One waits upstairs in one's room until called down | One greets them only if they have brought a gift
exam: Whereas a relative remarks "Look how much you have grown!", determine a fitting reply. | A smile and "Thank you" or "I know!" | Silence, with a turn of the head away | "That is a rude thing for you to say" | "You have grown a great deal older, too"
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a person's entitlement in respect of hugs and kisses from family members? | They may refuse, and others should respect it | They must accept, since the people are family | They may refuse only those from cousins | They must accept unless they are unwell
test: at the wedding auntie whispered "that's his second wife, the first one left" | He was married before and it ended; that's their business, so greet his wife politely | Go and tell his new wife all about the first wife, so that she knows the whole story too | Ask him why she left | Ignore his wife
test: my cousin said Uncle's "friend" coming to dinner is really "his girlfriend, shh" | Uncle may have a girlfriend; say hello politely and do not tease him | Ask her loudly at the dinner table if she is really his secret girlfriend | Tell everyone at the table | Refuse to say hello
test: kids at school said "Mr Brown n Miss Green walked in holding hands!" | They may be a couple; that is their private business, so greet them normally | It means they are lost | Shout it in the corridor | Ask them in front of the whole class whether they are going to get married soon
